\value{
An \code{R6} controller object with an \code{ssh} launcher.
}
\description{
Create an \code{R6} object to submit tasks and launch workers on a
remote system over \code{ssh}.
}
\details{
Remote workers connect back to the local \code{mirai} dispatcher through
a reverse \code{ssh} tunnel, so the local machine does not need to be reachable
from the remote system. The dispatcher listens on the loopback interface
by default and only the \code{ssh} connection leaves the machine.

The remote system needs R with the \code{crew} package installed, and the
\code{mirai} and \code{nanonext} versions on both ends should match. Any package a
task uses must be installed on the remote system too.
}
\section{Use with \code{targets}}{

Set \code{tar_option_set(storage = "main", retrieval = "main")} unless the
remote system mounts the same filesystem as the local one. The \code{targets}
default of \code{storage = "worker"} has each worker read and write
\verb{_targets/objects/} itself, which a worker on another machine cannot do:
the local process waits for files that never appear and the pipeline fails
with "File sync timed out". With \code{"main"}, target data travels over the
\code{ssh} tunnel along with the task. If the remote system does mount the
project directory, pass \code{directory} instead so workers start there and the
\code{targets} defaults keep working.
}

\section{Running workers in a container}{

\code{launch_prefix} runs each worker under another command, which is how a
worker runs inside a container instead of directly on the remote system:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{crew_controller_ssh(
  ssh_host = "user@example.com",
  launch_prefix = c(
    "docker", "run", "--init", "--rm", "--network=host",
    "--volume", "/tmp:/tmp", "my-image"
  )
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Three details of the surrounding design decide what the prefix has to
contain.

The reverse \code{ssh} tunnel listens on the \emph{remote system's} loopback
interface, and a container on a bridge network has a loopback of its own,
so a worker there would dial a port nothing is listening on. The
controller would then report a worker that launched but never connected.
The container must share the remote system's network namespace:
\code{--network=host} for \code{docker} and \code{podman}, which is already the default
for \code{apptainer}.

The launcher writes each worker's R script into \code{remote_directory} on the
remote system and the worker reads it back by that same path, so
\code{remote_directory} must be visible inside the container at the path it has
outside. Worker logs are written by the remote shell rather than by the
worker itself, so \code{logs()} keeps working whatever the prefix does. With
\code{salvage = TRUE} the worker also writes its results and records there.

Without \code{--init}, R is the first process of the container, and Linux
does not deliver a signal to that process unless it installed a handler
for it, which R does not for \code{SIGTERM}. A worker then ignores the
signal \code{mirai} sends it when its connection drops, keeps computing a
result nothing can collect, and outlives the session that started it.
\code{--init} makes a small init process the first process instead, which
passes the signal on. Use it (or an image whose entry point is an init
process such as \code{tini}).

When the prefix starts with \verb{docker run} or \verb{podman run}, the launcher
adds \code{--cidfile} to record each container's ID and stops a worker by
killing its container through the runtime. The process ID the launch
reports belongs to the runtime's client, and stopping the client alone
leaves the container running.
}

\section{Surviving a disconnect}{

Without salvage, remote workers exit as soon as their connection to the
controller drops, so a task in progress is lost when the local session
ends or the network goes down, and runs again from the start next time.
With \code{salvage = TRUE}, the default:
\enumerate{
\item A worker whose connection drops finishes the task in progress before
it exits.
\item Every worker saves the result of each task in \code{salvage_directory} on
the remote system before handing it back, writing to a temporary file
and renaming it, so a reader never sees a partial file. The record
holds the value or the error, the task's key, the worker, the remote
host, start and end times, and the versions of R and of every loaded
package.
\item When a later push of the same task, from this session or a later one,
finds a saved result, the worker hands that back instead of running
the task. It does so only for a task that succeeded and ran under the
same versions of R and of every package the worker has installed. An
error is never reused, and a saved file that cannot be read fails the
task with an error that names the file.
\item When the same task is still running on a worker of an earlier session,
the new worker attaches to it: it waits for that worker's result
instead of starting the task a second time, and hands the result back
when it appears. A running worker proves it is alive by touching a
heartbeat file every 15 seconds, even while R is busy. If a task's
worker dies without finishing, because it was killed, its container
was stopped, or the machine restarted, the heartbeat stops, and after
two minutes the new worker takes over and runs the task itself. While
this goes on, \code{remote_workers()} shows the old worker as \code{"running"}
and the new one as \code{"waiting"}. A waiting worker whose own session then
loses its connection can no longer hand the result back, so the next
session to open a tunnel to the same \code{remote_directory}, or the same
session when it reconnects, stops it with a message. The worker it
waited for still saves the result.
\item A task still running on a worker the controller cannot reach, one of
a session that is gone or one of this session from before its tunnel
was reopened, takes up the remote system as much as a connected
worker does, so it counts against \code{workers} until it finishes: the
controller launches only as many workers as are left, and says so.
Once the same task is pushed again, the worker that takes it holds
the slot instead, while it hands back the saved result or waits for
the running one. The controller reads the remote records for this at
most every 30 seconds.
}

Whether or not salvage is on, a failure of the network while the
controller reopens its tunnel or launches a worker is retried, with
pauses that grow from 5 to 30 seconds, until the pauses add up to
\code{seconds_reconnect}; only then is it an error. Time the local machine
spends asleep does not count, so a laptop that wakes before its network
does carries on once the network is back.

A task is identified by its name and by everything it computes from: the
command, data, globals, seed, and packages, or for a \code{targets} target,
its command, seed, settings, and the hashes of the data it depends on. A
task pushed without a name, as \code{push()} allows, runs without salvage.
Salvage assumes a task gives the same result each time it runs with the
same inputs, which \code{targets} arranges by seeding every target.

A saved result is deleted once a controller collects it with \code{pop()},
\code{collect()}, or \code{map()}. Results nothing collects stay until the
launcher's \code{salvage_remove()} deletes them; \code{salvage_list()} lists them.
The launcher's \code{remote_workers()} lists the workers of every session with
the task each is on, and \code{terminate_orphans()} stops the workers whose
session is gone.

Salvage writes every result to the remote disk until it is collected,
so it suits tasks that are slow to compute more than tasks that produce
a lot of data; \code{salvage = FALSE} turns it off. It cannot be combined
with custom \code{serialization}, which the saved files would not apply, so
a controller with \code{serialization} needs \code{salvage = FALSE}.
}

\examples{
if (identical(Sys.getenv("CREW_SSH_EXAMPLES"), "true")) {
controller <- crew_controller_ssh(ssh_host = "user@example.com")
controller$start()
controller$push(Sys.info()[["nodename"]])
controller$wait()
controller$pop()
controller$terminate()
}
}
